FYDE: drm/v3d: fix multi-client stalls and get the firmware mailbox off the submit path

With Chrome's GPU process and SurfaceFlinger both rendering, the whole
display would freeze for many seconds at a time, with no GPU reset and
nothing in dmesg.

The stall is not a GPU hang. Per-client fdinfo counters showed every
client's job count frozen at exactly the same instant and resuming
together, while CT0CS/CT1CS read 0 and CTnCA == CTnEA -- the hardware had
finished its last job and was idle. What was blocked was job *submission*.

v3d_clock_up_get() ran on every job submit and, under a device-global
mutex, called cancel_delayed_work_sync() and clk_set_min_rate(). The V3D
clock belongs to the VideoCore firmware, so each of those is a blocking
mailbox round trip on a single shared channel -- a channel CPU DVFS also
uses heavily (the ondemand governor was issuing ~115 transitions/s, each
one two more round trips). Every GPU client on the system therefore
serialised behind firmware mailbox latency. Pinning the CPU governor to
performance makes the stalls disappear entirely, which confirms it.

Submitters now only adjust an atomic refcount and kick a work item on a
dedicated ordered workqueue, which reconciles the rate asynchronously and
is the sole owner of ->clk_up (so clk_lock goes away). The idle delay
before dropping the clock goes from 100ms to 1s, since at 100ms a normal
interactive workload dropped and re-raised the clock in the gaps between
frames, generating continuous mailbox traffic for no benefit. The cost is
that the first job after an idle period may start before the clock has
been raised; the rate is a floor rather than the operating point, so that
is a small bounded effect and much better than stalling every client.

This is what the 'latest_job' serialisation in 035 was really doing: by
chaining every client's bin job behind the last job of any other client it
kept the pipeline continuously busy, so clk_refcount rarely reached zero
and the clock ping-pong stopped. It never actually serialised anything --
the read happened before v3d->sched_lock was taken and the publish after it
was dropped, so two concurrent submitters both saw the same predecessor --
it just suppressed mailbox traffic as a side effect. It has been dropped
from 035, along with the hunk in 040 that only existed to move
mutex_lock(&v3d->sched_lock) around it.

Fixed alongside, all genuine bugs found while tracking this down:

 - v3d_file_priv is now refcounted. v3d_postclose()'s guard
   (job->base.entity == &v3d_priv->sched_entity[q]) was dead code, because
   drm_sched_entity_pop_job() NULLs sched_job->entity before ->run_job()
   ever publishes the job. job->file_priv was therefore never cleared and
   the struct was freed while jobs were still in flight, letting the hard
   IRQ stats path write into freed slab. drm_sched_entity_destroy() only
   waits for jobs to be popped, not to complete, so closing an fd with work
   in flight is routine.
 - v3d_job_start_stats() dereferenced job->file_priv with no NULL check at
   all, unlike its sibling v3d_job_update_stats().
 - Bound the number of consecutive timeouts v3d_sched_skip_reset() will
   forgive. A job walking a corrupt control list keeps advancing CTnCA and
   so looks like it is progressing forever, blocking every other client
   with no reset and no log output.
 - Publish queue->active_job under queue_lock on the render/TFU/CSD queues,
   as the bin queue already did, and have the IRQ handler claim it under
   the same lock with a NULL guard for late or spurious interrupts.
 - Program CT0QMA/CT0QMS/CT0QTS unconditionally on V3D >= 4.1. They are
   sticky, so a job leaving any of them at zero inherited the previously
   running client's pointers.
 - Hold queue_lock across the BPOA/BPOS writes in v3d_overflow_mem_work()
   so the binner overflow pool cannot be handed to one job while being
   recorded on another's unref_list.
 - v3d_get_extensions() read an uninitialised ret once the default case
   stopped returning -EINVAL.
 - Move CPU-job teardown into the kref destructor. The ioctl error path was
   freeing the job and then reading cpu_job->timestamp_query.queries
   through it, and NULL-dereferencing when v3d_job_deallocate() had already
   zeroed it. Also release the indirect-CSD GEM reference, which nothing
   dropped, and stop leaking the indirect-CSD job on early error paths.
 - Destroy the clock work on teardown; it was never cancelled on remove.

